    id: 'r13', level: '大英赛', title: 'The Language of Numbers',
    meta: '约 285 词 · 大英赛风格 · 建议用时 8 分钟',
    body: [
      'Numbers seem to belong to the realm of pure reason, a universal language untouched by culture. Yet the way societies count, measure and talk about quantity reveals a great deal about how they think.',
      'Consider the number zero. Today it is indispensable to mathematics, but it was slow to gain acceptance. Ancient Greek mathematicians resisted it, in part because the idea of "nothing" as a value seemed philosophically troubling. The concept travelled from India through the Arab world before taking root in Europe, a reminder that even the simplest-seeming ideas have histories.',
      'Language shapes numerical thought in subtler ways. Some languages lack exact words for large numbers, using terms that mean roughly "many". Speakers of such languages are often less precise in estimating quantities, suggesting that vocabulary influences cognition rather than merely recording it.',
      'The framing of numbers also matters. A medical treatment described as having a "ninety percent survival rate" is judged more favourably than one with a "ten percent mortality rate", although the two statements are identical. Politicians and advertisers understand this well, and choose their phrasing accordingly.',
      'None of this means that numbers are meaningless or that mathematics is arbitrary. It means that numerical information reaches us through language and culture, and that a critical reader should attend not only to the figures themselves but to the words that carry them.'
    ],
